											<div id="mw-content-text" class="mw-body-content"><!-- Google Tag Manager (noscript) -->

<!-- End Google Tag Manager (noscript) -->
<div class="mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output" lang="en" dir="ltr"><div>
<div>
<figure class="article-media-thumbnail" data-attrs="{&quot;type&quot;:&quot;image&quot;,&quot;url&quot;:&quot;https:\/\/static.wikia.nocookie.net\/aesthetics\/images\/3\/34\/Rococo%2C_Urbancore_and_Minimalist_comparison.png\/revision\/latest?cb=20230730224832&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Rococo, Urbancore and Minimalist comparison.png&quot;,&quot;isLinkedByUser&quot;:true,&quot;href&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;isVideo&quot;:false,&quot;width&quot;:280,&quot;height&quot;:497,&quot;isMediaExternalSource&quot;:false}" data-file="Rococo,_Urbancore_and_Minimalist_comparison.png" data-linkedbyuser="true"> <img class="article-media-placeholder lazyautosizes lazyloaded" src="https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/3/34/Rococo%2C_Urbancore_and_Minimalist_comparison.png/revision/latest/scale-to-width-down/280?cb=20230730224832" data-src="https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/aesthetics/images/3/34/Rococo%2C_Urbancore_and_Minimalist_comparison.png/revision/latest/scale-to-width-down/280?cb=20230730224832" data-srcset="" data-sizes="auto" alt="Rococo, Urbancore and Minimalist comparison" width="280" height="497">  <figcaption> </figcaption> </figure>
<div>
<p>Aesthetics and styles manifest in music, architecture, furniture, ceramic, graphic design, interior design, illustration, painting, sculpture and art in general. These rows show how <b><a href="https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Rococo" title="Rococo">Rococo</a></b>, <b><a href="https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Urbancore" title="Urbancore">Urbancore</a></b>, and <b><a href="https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Minimalism" title="Minimalism">Minimalism</a></b> translate across different mediums. The first picture of each row shows an outfit that encapsulates that aesthetic, and the second row represents something else that belongs there.
</p>
</div>
</div>
<div>
<p><span>Need help finding your look?</span>
If you need assistance identifying your aesthetic or creating a moodboard, feel free to ask questions and seek inspiration from our community:
</p>
<ul><li>Visit our <a href="https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Special:Forum" title="Special:Forum">Discussion Tab</a> or <a target="_blank" rel="nofollow noreferrer noopener" class="external text" href="https://www.reddit.com/r/AestheticWiki/">subreddit</a> to post questions.</li>
<li>Join our <a target="_blank" rel="nofollow noreferrer noopener" class="external text" href="https://discord.com/invite/7vvddReyad">Official Discord Server</a> for real-time advice and feedback.</li>
<li>See <a href="https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Aesthetic_Visual_Index" title="Aesthetic Visual Index">Aesthetic Visual Index</a> and <a href="https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Find_Aesthetic_by_Image" title="Find Aesthetic by Image">Find Aesthetic by Image</a> for navigational visual aids.</li></ul>
</div>
<div>
<p><span>What is listed here?</span>
There is no single definition that captures the complexity of modern aesthetics. This list includes:
</p>
<ul><li><b>Internet Aesthetics:</b> Visual themes born on platforms like TikTok and Pinterest (e.g., <a href="https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Vaporwave" title="Vaporwave">Vaporwave</a>, <a href="https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Cottagecore" title="Cottagecore">Cottagecore</a>).</li>
<li><b>Fashion Styles:</b> Established modes of dress characterized by specific silhouettes and garments (e.g., <a href="https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Techwear" title="Techwear">Techwear</a>, <a href="https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Lolita" title="Lolita">Lolita</a>).</li>
<li><b>Subcultures:</b> Groups with shared fashion, music, and values (e.g., <a href="https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Goth" title="Goth">Goth</a>, <a href="https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Punk" title="Punk">Punk</a>).</li>
<li><b>Historical Eras:</b> Styles tied to specific time periods (e.g., <a href="https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Baroque" title="Baroque">Baroque</a>, <a href="https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Victorian" title="Victorian">Victorian</a>).</li>
<li><b>Music &amp; Fiction Genres:</b> Aesthetics related to music or narrative themes (e.g., <a href="https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Cyberpunk" title="Cyberpunk">Cyberpunk</a>, <a href="https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Dungeon_Synth" title="Dungeon Synth">Dungeon Synth</a>).<br></li></ul>
<div>Explore more in the <b><a href="https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Category:Aesthetics_by_Type" title="Category:Aesthetics by Type">Aesthetics by Type</a></b> category.</div>
</div>
